<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abd al-Rahman I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/731">731</a>–<a href="/years/788">788 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abd al-Rahman I was an Umayyad prince who escaped the massacre of his family during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-revolution">Abbasid Revolution</a> and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. In 756 he established an independent Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, which never recognised Abbasid authority. His survival meant that Umayyad rule continued in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> in the east, limiting the reach of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> in the west.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-i.jpg" alt="Abd al-Rahman I" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Numismática Pliego · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dirham_abd_al_rahman_i_21816.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abd al-Rahman ibn Mu'awiya was born on 7 March 731 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, the son of the Umayyad prince Mu'awiya ibn Hisham and the grandson of the caliph Hisham ibn Abd al-Malik. Later Andalusi historians name his mother as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> woman called Rah, but no contemporary source attests her. He was twenty when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-revolution">Abbasid Revolution</a> of 748 to 750 overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>. He fled with his brother Yahya, his young son Sulayman, some of his sisters and his Greek freedman Bedr, while Abbasid horsemen hunted the family. At the River Euphrates the horsemen called Yahya back with a promise of safety and killed him. Abd al-Rahman and Bedr escaped.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Abd al-Rahman became the first emir of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba</a> and ruled from 756 to 788. His rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> broke with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>, who had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, and he founded an Umayyad line that lasted nearly three centuries, including the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. He defeated a governor named Yusuf at Córdoba. He bought a standing army made up mainly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and of slaves from other areas, since he could not always rely on the local population, and he built up a civil service.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, Christians and other monotheists kept their faiths in exchange for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a>, as was usual in the early years of Islamic expansion. Scholars disagree on how fast the population became Muslim: one view is that the bulk of it did early, another that about 80 per cent converted but not until near the 10th century. Abd al-Rahman also improved the infrastructure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> and had the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba">Great Mosque of Córdoba</a> built in 785 to 787, which his successors enlarged until the 10th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Abd al-Rahman died in about 788 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> and was supposedly buried under the site of the Mezquita. He chose as his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-i-of-cordoba">Hisham I</a>, a son borne by a Spanish slave named Hulal. Another son, Sulayman, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, refused to accept Hisham's rule and was executed in 800 after challenging his nephew. Chroniclers report that the Abbasid caliph al-Mansur called Abd al-Rahman the Falcon of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a>, as one who rose alone, by his own judgement, from flight to a realm of his own. He stood against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> and other Muslim Spaniards, and his descendants ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> in the name of the house of Umayya, reaching their height under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abd al-Rahman I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shi'a supporters who had expected a dynasty descended from Ali felt betrayed, the Umayyad survivor <b>Abd al-Rahman I</b> escaped to establish a rival emirate in al-Andalus, and Abu Muslim himself remained a dangerous independent power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His dynasty descended from <b>Abd al-Rahman I</b>, a survivor of the Umayyad family overthrown by the Abbasids, who had founded an independent emirate at Córdoba in 756.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its most famous monument, the Great Mosque of Córdoba, was begun by <b>Abd al-Rahman I</b> around 785 and enlarged by Abd al-Rahman II and later rulers; its forest of columns and double arches of alternating red brick and white stone became a model for Islamic architecture in the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Abbasids massacred most of the Umayyad family; the most famous survivor, <b>Abd al-Rahman I</b>, escaped to Spain and founded the Emirate of Córdoba in 756.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>By the reign of <b>Abd al-Rahman I</b> it was absorbed into the Emirate of Córdoba, and in 825 Abd al-Rahman II founded Murcia as the capital of the province of Tudmir.</blockquote><a href="/state/tudmir">Tudmir</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. Died in Córdoba, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.51,36.29&amp;year=768" aria-label="Damascus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5477 948 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Abd al-Rahman I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5927 1218h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5927 1218h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.51,36.29&amp;year=768">What was here in 768 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
